1 NOTE {geni:about_me} History of Chen Dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT http://knows.jongo.com/res/article/4360
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Chen (557-589)
2 CONT
2 CONT Capital：Jiankang（建康）– present-day Nanjing of Jiangsu province 
2 CONT
2 CONT After forcing the last Liang emperor to hand over (chanrang 禅让) to him the heavenly mandate (tianming 天命), Chen Baxian陈霸先 (posthumously – Chen Wudi 陈武帝) inherited the imperial throne as Prince of Chen 陈. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the collapse of the Northern Wei 北魏 Empire, there was a new time of political unrest and military activity in the north and along the borders of Southern China. During the disturbances in the last decade of Liang, the northe
2 CONC rn empire – Northern Zhou 北周 – conquered the whole western part of Southern China, that is modern Sichuan and Yunnan. Chen Baxian’s empire was only half the size of that of the Liang dynasty. Furthermore, Chen Baxian was never abl
2 CONC e to fully control all territories of his empire as well as the political and military activities of the local gentry. It took him a long time to subdue all claimants to the throne from the Xiao family that had survived the claim 
2 CONC to power of the Chen clan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The most powerful person in the Chen empire after Chen Baxian’s death was Hou Andu 侯安都 who replaced the new emperor Chen Chang 陈昌 immediately with Chen Qian 陈倩 (posthumously – Chen Wendi 陈文帝). Chen Qian’s son Chen Bozong 陈伯宗 (post
2 CONC humously – Chen Feidi 陈废帝，The Deposed Emperor) was toppled by his own uncle Chen Xu 陈顼 (posthumously – Chen Xuandi 陈宣帝). Chen Xu’s reign was a relatively calm and peaceful period, except for the few unsuccessful military campaigns
2 CONC  of Wu Mingche 吴明彻 against the North. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, at the northern frontier a serious change had taken place. The Northern Zhou empire 北周 had conquered the neighboring state, Northern Qi 北齐 and now controlled Northern and Western China. Thereupon Northern Zhou was destr
2 CONC oyed by general Yang Jian 杨坚 who founded the Sui Dynasty 隋. He then conquered the small state of Chen in the south and reunited China under his rule. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emperor_Wu_of_Chen
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Wu of Chen (陳武帝) (503-559), personal name Chen Baxian (陳霸先), courtesy name Xingguo (興國), nickname Fasheng (法生), was the first emperor of the Chinese Chen Dynasty. He first distinguished himself as a Liang Dynasty general d
2 CONC uring the campaign against the rebel general Hou Jing, and he was progressively promoted. In 555, he seized power after a coup against his superior, the general Wang Sengbian, and in 557 he forced Emperor Jing to yield the throne 
2 CONC to him, establishing Chen Dynasty. He died in 559, and as his only surviving son Chen Chang was held by Northern Zhou as a hostage, he was succeeded by his nephew Chen Qian (Emperor Wen).
2 CONT
2 CONT Background and early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen Baxian was born in 503, the second year of the reign of Emperor Wu of Liang (the founding emperor of Liang Dynasty). He was from Wuxing Commandery (吳興, roughly modern Huzhou, Zhejiang). His family traced its ancestry to Chen 
2 CONC Shi (陳寔), a county magistrate and Confucian scholar during Han Dynasty. During the lineage that was traced, Chen's ancestors generally served as low-level officials, although several were important figures in imperial governments 
2 CONC of Jin Dynasty and the subsequent Southern dynasties, including Chen Baxian's grandfather Chen Daoju (陳道巨). However, no record indicated that Chen Baxian's father Chen Wenzan (陳文讚) was an official. His mother was a Lady Dong, prob
2 CONC ably Chen Wenzan's wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Chen Baxian was young, he was considered ambitious, not caring about managing properties. As he grew, he studied military strategies and learned various fighting techniques. Initially, he married a daughter of Qian Zhongfang 
2 CONC (錢仲方), who was also from Wuxing Commandery, but she died early. After Lady Qian's death, he married Zhang Yao'er, likewise from Wuxing Commandery. She bore him at least one son, Chen Chang. (It is known that he had five sons befor
2 CONC e Chen Chang, but all, including Chen Ke (陳克), the only one whose name is preserved in history, appeared to have died early; it is not known who were their mothers.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In the late 530s, when Xiao Ying (蕭映) the Marquess of Xinyu, a nephew of Emperor Wu, was the governor of Wuxing Commandery, he had the chance to see Chen Baxian and was impressed by him. When Xiao Ying was made the governor of Gua
2 CONC ng Province (廣州, modern Guangdong) around 540, he invited Chen to serve on his staff, and subsequently, Xiao Ying made him an acting commandery governor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 541, the people of Jiao Province (交州, modern northern Vietnam), dissatisfied with the cruel rule of Xiao Zi (蕭諮) the Marquess of Wulin (another nephew of Emperor Wu), rebelled under the leadership of Li Ben. Xiao Zi fled to Gua
2 CONC ng Province. Xiao Ying sent the generals Sun Jiong (孫冏) and Lu Zixiong (盧子雄) to attack Li, with Xiao Ying overseeing the operations. In spring 542, Xiao Ying and Xiao Zi ordered Sun and Lu to attack, despite Sun and Lu's request t
2 CONC o delay the attack to fall 542 due to fears that hot temperature could cause illnesses. Li crushed their forces with heavy casualties, and Xiao Zi falsely accused Sun and Lu of working in concern with Li, and Emperor Wu ordered Su
2 CONC n and Lu to commit suicide. Lu Zixiong's brothers Lu Zilüe (盧子略) and Lu Zilie (盧子烈) and subordinates, the brothers Du Tianhe (杜天合) and Du Sengming (杜僧明) and Zhou Wenyu (周文育) attacked the capital of Guang Province, wanting to kill 
2 CONC Xiao Zi and Xiao Ying to avenge Lu Zixiong. Xiao Ying ordered Chen to engage them, and he defeated them, killing Du Tianhe and capturing Du Sengming and Zhou. Believing that Du Sengming and Zhou were both good soldiers, he release
2 CONC d them and retained them on his staff. For this accomplishment, Emperor Wu created Chen the Viscount of Xin'an, and while he did not summon Chen to the capital Jiankang, he had an artisan draw a portrait of Chen and deliver it to 
2 CONC him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In winter 544, Xiao Ying died, and initially, Chen started escorting Xiao Ying's casket back to Jiankang for burial. On the way, while he was still at Dayu Mountain (大庾嶺, on the borders of modern Jiangxi and Guangdong), he was ord
2 CONC ered to rendezvous with the new governor of Jiao Province, Yang Piao (楊瞟), and another nephew of Emperor Wu's, Xiao Bo (蕭勃), to attack Li. Xiao Bao did not want to set out on the campaign, and therefore try to persuade Yang not to
2 CONC  advance. Chen persuaded Yang otherwise, and in spring 545, Yang, with Chen as his lieutenant, attacked Li, defeating him and forcing him flee into the mountains and conduct guerilla warfare instead. In 548, Li's subordinates kill
2 CONC ed Li, and when Li's brother Li Tianbao succeeded him and attacked Ai Province (愛州, centering modern Thanh Hóa), Chen defeated Li Tianbao. Emperor Wu made Chen the governor of Gaoyao Commandery (高要, roughtly modern Zhaoqing, Guang
2 CONC dong) as well as the commander of the forces of the surrounding commanderies.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] During the Hou Jing Disturbance
2 CONT
2 CONT In summer 548, the general Hou Jing, formerly an Eastern Wei (a branch successor state of Northern Wei) general that Emperor Wu had accepted the defection of, rebelled, and in 549 captured Jiankang, taking Emperor Wu and his son a
2 CONC nd crown prince Xiao Gang hostage. After Jiankang's fall, Hou, who had initially claimed that he wanted to restore Northern Wei's imperial clan to power, from the control of the regent Gao Cheng, enticed the governor of Guang Prov
2 CONC ince, Yuan Jingzhong (元景仲), a member of Northern Wei's imperial Yuan clan, to join him, and when Chen received the news, he publicly announced Yuan's treachery and gathered the troops of the nearby generals to attack Yuan. Yuan co
2 CONC mmitted suicide, and Chen welcomed Xiao Bo, then the governor of Ding Province (定州, roughly modern Guigang, Guangxi) to take over Guang Province. In winter 549, against Xiao Bo's request, Chen took his troops and embarked on a cam
2 CONC paign to join the fight against Hou, sending messengers to Emperor Wu's son Xiao Yi the Prince of Xiangdong, the governor of Jing Province (荊州, modern central and western Hubei), pledging support and loyalty to Xiao Yi, then commo
2 CONC nly viewed as the leader of the remaining Liang provinces not under Hou's control.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the next year, Chen advanced north through modern Jiangxi, fighting the various local warlords and generals loyal to Hou, with his main struggle against Li Qianshi (李遷仕). In spring 551, he captured and killed Li. Xiao Yi made 
2 CONC him the governor of Jiang Province (江州, roughly modern Jiangxi). By fall 551, he had rendezvoused with Xiao Yi's main general, Wang Sengbian, at Xunyang (尋陽, in modern Jiujiang, Jiangxi). In 552, after they had sworn a solemn oath
2 CONC  to Liang, they advanced east toward Jiankang, where Hou had killed Xiao Gang (who had succeeded Emperor Wu as Emperor Jianwen) and taken the throne himself as Emperor of Han. Chen was instrumental in the subsequent siege of Jiank
2 CONC ang, and they defeated Hou together, causing Hou to flee. Subsequently, Hou was killed by his own men. For Chen's contributions, Xiao Yi created Chen the Marquess of Changcheng -- Chen's home county. Wang put Chen in charge of the
2 CONC  important city Jingkou (京口, in modern Zhenjiang, Jiangsu). For the next two years, Chen was several times involved in border battles against Northern Qi (Eastern Wei's successor state). At times, when Xiao Yi (who had by now take
2 CONC n the throne as Emperor Yuan but set up his capital at his headquarters of Jiangling rather than at Jiankang) summoned Wang on campaigns, Wang would put Chen in charge of Jiankang.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Seizure of power
2 CONT
2 CONT In 554, Western Wei launched a major attack on Jiangling, and Emperor Yuan summoned Wang to come to his aid, putting Chen in charge of Jiankang. Before Wang could reach Jiangling, however, Western Wei had already captured Jianglin
2 CONC g, killing Emperor Yuan and declaring his nephew Xiao Cha emperor instead (as Emperor Xuan). Wang and Chen refused to recognize Emperor Xuan; instead, in spring 555, they welcomed Emperor Yuan's 11-year-old son Xiao Fangzhi the Pr
2 CONC ince of Jin'an -- Emperor Yuan's only surviving son -- to Jiankang, preparing to make him emperor and first having him take the title Prince of Liang. (When Jiankang fell, Chen's son Chen Chang and nephew Chen Xu, who had been ser
2 CONC ving in the imperial administration, were captured and taken to the Western Wei capital Chang'an as honored captives.)
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, however, Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi had other ideas, and he sent his brother Gao Huan (高渙) the Prince of Shangdang to command an army to escort Emperor Yuan's cousin Xiao Yuanming the Marquess of Zhenyang -- whom
2 CONC  Eastern Wei had taken captive in 547 -- back to Liang to be emperor. Wang initially rejected Xiao Yuanming, but after his forces lost a few battles to Northern Qi forces, changed his mind and decided to accept Xiao Yuanming as em
2 CONC peror after extracting a promise from Xiao Yuanming to make Xiao Fangzhi crown prince. In summer 555, Xiao Yuanming arrived at Jiankang to take the throne, and he created Xiao Fangzhi crown prince. Wang and Chen continued to be in
2 CONC  charge of the military.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen, however, was unhappy about the situation, believing Xiao Yuanming to be unworthy of the throne. Despite Wang Sengbian's knowledge of Chen's displeasure, however, Wang did not suspect Chen of having any rebellious intentions,
2 CONC  as they had been friendly, and Wang and Chen had agreed on having Wang Sengbian's son Wang Wei (王頠) marry Chen's daughter, although the marriage had not been established on account of the recent death of Wang Sengbian's mother. i
2 CONC n fall 555, believing reports that Northern Qi was going to attack, Wang sent his secretary Jiang Gan (江旰) to Jingkou to alert Chen. Chen instead detained Jiang and started a surprise attack on Wang. With Wang not suspecting that 
2 CONC an attack would occur, Chen quickly reached Wang's headquarters at Shitou (a fortress near Jiankang), capturing and killing Wang Sengbian and Wang Wei. He took over control of the imperial government, forcing Xiao Yuanming to abdi
2 CONC cate and making Xiao Fangzhi emperor (as Emperor Jing).
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately, Chen faced resistance from the generals Xu Sihui (徐嗣徽), Ren Yue (任約), and Hou Tian (侯瑱), and Wang Sengbian's brother Wang Sengzhi (王僧智) and son-in-law Du Kan (杜龕). (Du was the governor of Chen's home commandery of Wux
2 CONC ing, and the historian Bo Yang had speculated that Du's disrespect for Chen and his curbing of special privileges that Chen's clan was exerting in Wuxing might have been an impetus fo Chen's rebellion against Wang.) Chen initially
2 CONC  sent his nephew Chen Qian and his general Zhou Wenyu against Du and Du's ally Wei Zai (韋載), but the campaign was inconclusive, and Chen subsequently went to attack himself. Meanwhile, Xu and Ren, aided by Northern Qi, made a surp
2 CONC rise attack on Jiankang, nearly capturing it, but were repelled by Chen's general Hou Andu. Soon, Chen defeated Wei and Wei surrendered, and Chen returned to Jiankang, leaving Zhou to face Du.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Northern Qi aid, Xu and Ren could not defeat Chen, and Chen put Shitou, which the Northern Qi general Liu Damo (柳達摩) had captured, under siege. Liu sought peace, but requested Chen to send his relatives as hostages to Nort
2 CONC hern Qi. Most officials advocated peace, and Chen, despite his skepticism about such a peace holding, agreed, and sent his nephew Chen Tanlang (陳曇朗), Emperor Yuan's grandson Xiao Zhuang the Prince of Yongjia, and Wang Min (王珉), th
2 CONC e son of the key official Wang Chong (王沖), as hostages, permitting Northern Qi forces to withdraw, and Xu and Ren withdrew with them.
2 CONT
2 CONT By spring 556, Du had either been captured or surrendered to Zhou and Chen Qian, and Chen Baxian executed Du. Wang Sengzhi fled to Northern Qi, and the capital region was largely under Chen Baxian's control. Meanwhile, Northern Qi
2 CONC  forces were preparing another attack, but they invited Xiao Yuanming to their camp to discuss peace. Chen sent Xiao Yuanming to Northern Qi camp, but before talks could began, Xiao Yuanming died from a severe infection on his bac
2 CONC k. By summer 556, Northern Qi forces were again descending on Jiankang, but once there, their forces stalemated with Chen's forces. Northern Qi forces' food supplies soon ran out, and Chen defeated them, killing Xu and capturing a
2 CONC  number of Northern Qi generals, whom Chen executed. (In response, Northern Qi executed Chen Tanlang, although Chen Baxian never found out during his life time.) Meanwhile, Hou Tian, having been defeated by another general, Hou Pi
2 CONC ng (侯平), chose to submit to Chen.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the next year, Chen began to receive greater and greater titles and offices, progressing from being the Marquess of Changcheng to Duke of Changcheng to Duke of Yixing to Duke of Chen to Prince of Chen. In 557, Xiao Bo decla
2 CONC red a resistance against Chen from Guang Province. Soon, however, Zhou defeated Xiao Bo's general Ouyang Wei (歐陽頠), and Xiao Bo was killed by his own generals. At the same time, Wang Lin, who controlled modern Hunan and eastern Hu
2 CONC bei, suspicious of Chen's intentions, refused his summon to Jiankang and prepared for battle instead. Chen sent Zhou and Hou Andu against Wang Lin. In winter 557, Chen had Emperor Jing yield the throne to him, establishing Chen Dy
2 CONC nasty as its Emperor Wu. He created Emperor Jing the Prince of Jiangyin. He posthumously honored his parents emperor and empress, his deceased wife Lady Qian empress, and his deceased son Chen Ke crown prince. He created his wife 
2 CONC Zhang Yao'er empress.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT While it is not known when Emperor Wu became a Buddhist, once he became emperor he immediately took steps to officially sanction Buddhism, as he displayed a relic believed to be a Buddha's tooth and held a major Buddhist festival.
2 CONC  He also, following the lead of Liang's Emperor Wu, offered himself to Buddha's service on one occasion. He made several requests to Western Wei's successor state Northern Zhou to return Chen Chang and Chen Xu, and while Northern 
2 CONC Zhou promised to do so, they would actually not be returned in Emperor Wu's lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, news that Emperor Wu had accepted the throne had reached the front where Zhou Wenyu and Hou Andu were engaging Wang Lin, greatly depressing Zhou and Hou's forces, as this removed a major appeal that they had -- that Wan
2 CONC g was being a rebel for refusing to follow Emperor Jing's orders. Wang defeated Zhou and Hou and captured them. After doing so, however, both Chen forces and Wang Lin's forces were stalemated by the fact that the general Lu Xida (
2 CONC 魯悉達), who controlled Northern Jiang Province (北江州, roughly modern Anqing, Anhui), was accepting overtures from both sides but refusing to actually obey either side. Not able to make progress in his campaign against the new Chen st
2 CONC ate, Wang sought help from Northern Qi and requested that it return Xiao Zhuang to be emperor. Soon, Northern Qi returned Xiao Zhuang, and Wang Lin declared Xiao Zhuang emperor at Ying Province (郢州, modern eastern Hubei).
2 CONT
2 CONT In summer 558, Emperor Wu had the former Emperor Jing of Liang killed. He sent Hou Tian and Xu Du (徐度) to attack Wang Lin, but soon negotiated a peace with Wang Lin, after Wang's general Yu Xiaoqing (余孝頃) was defeated by the indep
2 CONC endent general Zhou Di (周迪). (Meanwhile, Zhou Wenyu and Hou escaped from Wang's custody and returned to Chen, although Zhou was soon assassinated by the independent general Xiong Tanlang (熊曇朗).)
2 CONT
2 CONT In summer 559, Emperor Wu suffered a major illness and died suddenly. At that time, the only close relative of his in Chen territory, his nephew Chen Qian the Prince of Linchuan, was away building a fort at Nanhuan (南皖, in modern 
2 CONC Anqing, Anhui). Empress Zhang, after consulting the officials Du Leng (杜稜) and Cai Jingli (蔡景歷), chose not to announce Emperor Wu's death and summoned Chen Qian back from Nanhuan. The imperial officials decided to support Chen Qia
2 CONC n as emperor, and while Empress Zhang was initially hesitant, hoping that Chen Chang would return, she eventually agreed, and Chen Qian took the throne as Emperor Wen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Era name
2 CONT
2 CONT Yongding (永定 yǒng dìng) 557-559 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Personal information
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen Wenzan (陳文讚), posthumously honored as Emperor Jing 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Dong, probably Chen Wenzan's wife, posthumously honored as Empress An 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Qian, daughter of Qian Zhongfang (錢仲方), posthumously honored as Empress Zhao 
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress Zhang Yao'er (created 557), mother of Prince Chang 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen Ke (陳克), died early, posthumously honored as Crown Prince Xiaohuai 
2 CONT
2 CONT Four other sons who died early 
2 CONT
2 CONT Chen Chang (陳昌), Prince Xian of Hengyang (created and killed by Emperor Wen of Chen 560) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Mu of Kuaiji 
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Yongsi 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://cbdb.fas.harvard.edu/cbdbapi/person.php?id=12828 '''Chen Baxian''' 陳霸先] [12828] Founder of the Chen dynasty as Chen Wudi. Renming Da cidian, p. 1077. See also ancestor Zhen(7)'s [12821] documentation. Giles, p. 96. — [htt
2 CONC ps://www.geni.com/projects/CBDB-Hartwell-collection/46406 RMH]
